<Project>

  <!--
    Stamps the REAL build time onto an assembly, as semver build metadata on
    AssemblyInformationalVersion (issue #136). Imported by the two projects the About dialog
    reports on: the VSIX shell and the engine it bundles.

    Why it exists: the dialog used to report File.GetLastWriteTime of the deployed files, which
    is when they were INSTALLED, not when they were built. Measured on this box - an Exp deploy
    stamped every file in the extension folder, shell and bundled engine alike, with one
    identical time - so the two rows could never differ from each other, neither was a build
    time, and the "is the bundled engine stale?" question they exist to answer was unanswerable
    by construction. Only a stamp put in at compile time survives being copied.

    Why InformationalVersion rather than an [AssemblyMetadata] attribute: the engine is net10
    and the dialog is net472, so it can never load the engine assembly to read attributes off
    it. InformationalVersion lands in the Win32 version resource as ProductVersion, which
    FileVersionInfo reads straight off a file without loading it - so ONE mechanism serves both
    rows, and the value is visible in the file's own Properties page as a bonus.

    Format: <version>+<sha>.<yyyyMMddTHHmmZ>[.ci] - dot-separated build-metadata identifiers,
    which is what semver allows after '+'. Hence the compact timestamp: ':' is not legal there.
    The sha half is the SDK's own doing (AddSourceRevisionToInformationalVersion); '.ci' marks a
    build produced by the release workflow, which is what separates an official build from a
    local one of the same commit.

    DependsOnTargets rather than a second BeforeTargets hook: two targets both hooked to
    GetAssemblyAttributes have no guaranteed order between them, and this one must run AFTER the
    sha has been appended or it would compose the identifiers in the wrong order (and the
    Contains('+') branch below would pick wrong).

    Cost: the value changes every build, so these two projects recompile on every build - and
    the VSIX repackages with them. Measured at ~1.1s over a no-op solution build (1.85s ->
    2.99s), against a bug report that otherwise cannot say which build it is about.
  -->
  <Target Name="StampBuildTimestamp"
          BeforeTargets="GetAssemblyAttributes"
          DependsOnTargets="AddSourceRevisionToInformationalVersion">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_BuildStampUtc>$([System.DateTime]::UtcNow.ToString('yyyyMMdd\THHmm\Z'))</_BuildStampUtc>
      <!-- GITHUB_ACTIONS is set by the runner itself, so nothing has to be passed through. -->
      <_BuildStampCi Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">.ci</_BuildStampCi>
      <InformationalVersion Condition="$(InformationalVersion.Contains('+'))">$(InformationalVersion).$(_BuildStampUtc)$(_BuildStampCi)</InformationalVersion>
      <InformationalVersion Condition="!$(InformationalVersion.Contains('+'))">$(InformationalVersion)+$(_BuildStampUtc)$(_BuildStampCi)</InformationalVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Message Importance="normal" Text="Build stamp: $(InformationalVersion)" />
  </Target>

</Project>
